<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Counter-Reformation</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1545–<a href="/years/1648">1648</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Catholic Reformation</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Counter-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> was the period of Catholic renewal and response to the Protestant Reformation, from the mid-16th to the mid-17th century. Its programme was set by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-trent">Council of Trent</a> (1545–1563), which defined doctrine and reformed the clergy, and it was carried forward by new orders such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a>, by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-inquisition">Inquisition</a> and by Catholic rulers who restored their faith in their lands. It also shaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> art and architecture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/counter-reformation.jpg" alt="Counter-Reformation" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bartolomé Esteban Murillo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bartolom%C3%A9_Esteban_Murillo,_Faith_or_The_Church_Triumphant_as,_165_x_251_cm.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The term Counter-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> translates the German Gegenreformation, coined in the late eighteenth century by German historians as a negative concept for Catholic repression and popularised by writers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-von-ranke">Leopold von Ranke</a>. Protestant historians have tended to treat Catholic reform as part of it. The period is frequently dated from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-trent">Council of Trent</a> (1545 to 1563) to the end of the European wars of religion in 1648, though this dating is controversial. Catholic reform had earlier roots in the Gregorian reforms of the late 11th century, the Benedictine Bull of 1336 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capuchins">Capuchins</a>, who arose from Matteo de Bascio's proposal of 1526 to return to the original Franciscan rule.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-paul-iii">Pope Paul III</a>, considered the first pope of the Counter-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>, initiated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-trent">Council of Trent</a>, which dealt with corrupt clergy and the sale of indulgences. The council upheld the medieval church's sacramental system and doctrine and rejected compromise with Protestants. Pius V made the Tridentine Mass obligatory in 1570. Other measures included the Roman Catechism of 1566, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/index-librorum-prohibitorum">Index Librorum Prohibitorum</a> of 1559 and seminaries to train priests, and new orders such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a>, Discalced Carmelites and Ursulines arose. The effort also used regional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-inquisition">Inquisitions</a> and state power, and had a missionary side in lands colonised by Catholic nations.</p><p>Rudolf II began suppressing Protestant activity in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> lands in 1576. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, Catholics and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a> fought wars from 1562, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edict-of-nantes">Edict of Nantes</a> of 1598 brought peace; in 1685 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> abolished the right of Huguenots to worship.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Counter-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> drastically diminished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> lands, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, but did less in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. War and discriminatory laws caused large migrations of religious refugees, and towns such as Freudenstadt and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gluckstadt">Glückstadt</a> were founded as homes for them. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-brest">Union of Brest</a> in 1596, Ruthenian Orthodox bishops entered full communion with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> while keeping their Byzantine liturgy and married clergy. The Church was also a leading patron of the arts, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> style of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gian-lorenzo-bernini">Bernini</a>'s Rome and Rubens's Flanders served to restore Catholic centrality.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Counter-Reformation” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Society remained strongly Catholic: the dukes rejected the Reformation, brought the Jesuits to Ingolstadt in the 1550s and made Bavaria a centre of the <b>Counter-Reformation</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-bavaria">Duchy of Bavaria · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Spanish rule the economy suffered from heavy taxation and the plague of 1630, while the <b>Counter-Reformation</b> archbishop Charles Borromeo reshaped parish life.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Duchy of Milan · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Counter-Reformation</b> produced a pervasive Baroque piety of pilgrimages, monasteries and brotherhoods.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-monarchy">Habsburg Monarchy · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>It was the chief power of the Catholic <b>Counter-Reformation</b>: Ignatius of Loyola founded the Society of Jesus, Teresa of Ávila and John of the Cross reformed the Carmelites, and Spanish theologians shaped the Council of Trent.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-spain">Habsburg Spain · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>After 1620 the <b>Counter-Reformation</b> restored a Catholic monopoly, and a new, partly foreign nobility built baroque estates worked by serfs, whose labour obligations remained heavy until Joseph II abolished personal serfdom in 1781.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Kingdom of Bohemia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>After 1620 Catholicism was restored by force, with the Jesuit university of Olomouc, founded in 1573, as a centre of the <b>Counter-Reformation</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-moravia">Margraviate of Moravia · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
